39:       {icon:'🗺️',title:'A map beats a guess',text:'Regions are places with histories, languages and changing borders—not boxes that neatly sort people. Look for context instead of trying to read identity from someone’s appearance.',contrast:'Try this: compare the geography, place names and languages mentioned in a clue. Those details point to a place; they never tell you everything about a person.'},
40:       {icon:'🗣️',title:'One country, many languages',text:'National borders rarely match language borders. People may speak a local, Indigenous, national or family language—and switch between several in daily life.',contrast:'Oaxaca has many Indigenous languages alongside Spanish. In Québec, French is official, while English and Indigenous languages are also part of the province’s story.'},
41:       {icon:'🌊',title:'Nearby places can differ',text:'Coasts, mountains, trade routes and migration all shape local histories. Neighboring regions can share a lot and still have distinct languages and traditions.',contrast:'Jeju’s island geography shaped a distinctive local history. Compare it with mainland Korea using place and language clues, not assumptions about people.'},
42:       {icon:'🍲',title:'Traditions are not stereotypes',text:'Food, festivals, music and clothing are meaningful—but no tradition belongs to every person in a region. Communities change, overlap and create new traditions.',contrast:'Flamenco has deep roots in Andalusia and Romani culture, with many influences. It is an art form—not a shortcut for identifying anyone.'}
